1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,338
If you've heard that the magic number for a federal single audit is seven hundred and fifty thousand dollars

2
00:00:06,338 --> 00:00:08,098
hold on, because that changed.

3
00:00:08,098 --> 00:00:10,622
The number that matters now is one million.

4
00:00:10,622 --> 00:00:13,028
And if your nonprofit is anywhere near it

5
00:00:13,028 --> 00:00:19,365
that difference decides whether you're about to spend the next few months in an audit you didn't budget for.

6
00:00:19,365 --> 00:00:21,243
So let's clear this up properly.

7
00:00:21,243 --> 00:00:25,234
In the next ten minutes I'll explain what a single audit actually is

8
00:00:25,234 --> 00:00:29,224
who is required to get one, the exact dollar figure that triggers it

9
00:00:29,224 --> 00:00:31,806
and what the auditor does once you're in it.

10
00:00:31,806 --> 00:00:35,151
No jargon for its own sake, and no pitch to hire anybody.

11
00:00:35,151 --> 00:00:37,264
Quick note on where I'm coming from.

12
00:00:37,264 --> 00:00:39,200
I build grant compliance software

13
00:00:39,200 --> 00:00:44,540
so my job is to model these federal rules precisely enough that a computer can follow them.

14
00:00:44,540 --> 00:00:47,768
I'm not an auditor, and I'm not going to talk like one.

15
00:00:47,768 --> 00:00:51,641
You're getting the version a busy finance director actually needs.

16
00:00:51,641 --> 00:00:56,032
Let's start with what the thing even is, because the name is misleading.

17
00:00:56,032 --> 00:00:59,690
A single audit is not a small audit, and it's not one audit.

18
00:00:59,690 --> 00:01:06,398
It's called "single" because it rolls what used to be a pile of separate agency audits into one yearly review.

19
00:01:06,398 --> 00:01:08,228
A single audit has two halves.

20
00:01:08,228 --> 00:01:13,594
The first half is the part you might already know: an audit of your financial statements

21
00:01:13,594 --> 00:01:16,948
the normal question of whether your books are accurate.

22
00:01:16,948 --> 00:01:19,814
The second half is the one that catches people.

23
00:01:19,814 --> 00:01:22,619
It's a compliance audit of your federal money.

24
00:01:22,619 --> 00:01:25,241
Did you spend it on what you said you would

25
00:01:25,241 --> 00:01:30,302
did you follow the rules attached to each award, and can you prove it with records.

26
00:01:30,302 --> 00:01:32,315
That second half is the real work

27
00:01:32,315 --> 00:01:39,266
and it's why a single audit is a much bigger deal than the financial audit many nonprofits already get every year.

28
00:01:39,266 --> 00:01:41,706
The rules come from the Single Audit Act

29
00:01:41,706 --> 00:01:46,096
which Congress passed in nineteen eighty-four and amended in ninety-six.

30
00:01:46,096 --> 00:01:49,694
Today they live in a regulation called the Uniform Guidance

31
00:01:49,694 --> 00:01:54,999
specifically Title two of the Code of Federal Regulations, Part two hundred, Subpart F.

32
00:01:54,999 --> 00:02:00,792
If you've been doing this a while and you remember something called Circular A-one-thirty-three

33
00:02:00,792 --> 00:02:02,195
that's the old version.

34
00:02:02,195 --> 00:02:03,414
Same idea, new home.

35
00:02:03,414 --> 00:02:07,988
When somebody on your board says "the A-133 audit," this is what they mean.

36
00:02:07,988 --> 00:02:09,966
So who actually has to do this.

37
00:02:09,966 --> 00:02:12,008
Here's the rule in one sentence.

38
00:02:12,008 --> 00:02:18,453
If your organization spends one million dollars or more in federal awards during a single fiscal year

39
00:02:18,453 --> 00:02:21,898
you are required to have a single audit for that year.

40
00:02:21,898 --> 00:02:25,791
That one-million-dollar figure is the headline, and it's new.

41
00:02:25,791 --> 00:02:29,875
For years the threshold sat at seven hundred and fifty thousand.

42
00:02:29,875 --> 00:02:33,958
The 2024 update to the Uniform Guidance raised it to one million

43
00:02:33,958 --> 00:02:39,127
and that change takes effect for fiscal years beginning on or after October first

44
00:02:39,127 --> 00:02:40,339
twenty twenty-four.

45
00:02:40,339 --> 00:02:41,233
In plain terms

46
00:02:41,233 --> 00:02:46,656
for most organizations that means fiscal years ending on or after September thirtieth

47
00:02:46,656 --> 00:02:47,869
twenty twenty-five.

48
00:02:47,869 --> 00:02:52,846
So a lot of the advice still floating around online is quoting the old number.

49
00:02:52,846 --> 00:02:58,908
If a video or a checklist tells you the line is seven hundred fifty thousand, it's out of date.

50
00:02:58,908 --> 00:03:00,630
One more piece of the "who.

51
00:03:00,630 --> 00:03:05,161
" The single audit applies to what the rules call non-federal entities.

52
00:03:05,161 --> 00:03:10,457
That's nonprofits, states, local governments, tribal governments, and universities.

53
00:03:10,457 --> 00:03:13,967
It does not apply to for-profit companies the same way.

54
00:03:13,967 --> 00:03:18,433
If you're a 501(c)(3) spending federal money, you're squarely in scope

55
00:03:18,433 --> 00:03:21,177
and the threshold is that same one million.

56
00:03:21,177 --> 00:03:25,799
Now the part people get wrong, and it's the most expensive thing to misread.

57
00:03:25,799 --> 00:03:28,535
The trigger is not how much you were awarded.

58
00:03:28,535 --> 00:03:31,272
It's not how much cash hit your bank account.

59
00:03:31,272 --> 00:03:35,954
It's how much you expended, how much you actually spent, in your fiscal year.

60
00:03:35,954 --> 00:03:37,900
This matters in both directions.

61
00:03:37,900 --> 00:03:45,319
You could be awarded a two-million-dollar grant that runs over three years and never trip the threshold in any single year

62
00:03:45,319 --> 00:03:47,934
because you only spend a portion each year.

63
00:03:47,934 --> 00:03:55,110
Or you could draw down several smaller grants quickly and cross one million without any single award looking that big.

64
00:03:55,110 --> 00:03:59,671
The rule looks at the total federal money you spent in the year, full stop.

65
00:03:59,671 --> 00:04:06,238
So the question to ask every year is not "how big are my grants," it's "how much federal money did we spend.

66
00:04:06,238 --> 00:04:13,171
" And spending counts whether the money came straight from a federal agency or passed through somebody else first.

67
00:04:13,171 --> 00:04:17,914
If a state agency or another nonprofit hands you federal dollars as a subaward

68
00:04:17,914 --> 00:04:20,225
that still counts toward your million.

69
00:04:20,225 --> 00:04:24,360
A lot of organizations forget the pass-through money and undercount.

70
00:04:24,360 --> 00:04:30,016
The one thing that doesn't count is money you earn as a contractor selling goods or services.

71
00:04:30,016 --> 00:04:34,637
So you add up your direct federal awards and your federal pass-through money

72
00:04:34,637 --> 00:04:37,617
and that combined number is what you're watching.

73
00:04:37,617 --> 00:04:43,419
There's one fork in the road worth knowing, because it can save a smaller organization real money.

74
00:04:43,419 --> 00:04:47,444
The default, if you're over the threshold, is the full single audit.

75
00:04:47,444 --> 00:04:51,115
But there's a narrower option called a program-specific audit.

76
00:04:51,115 --> 00:04:57,627
You can elect the program-specific version only if you spent your federal money under a single federal program

77
00:04:57,627 --> 00:04:58,278
not several

78
00:04:58,278 --> 00:05:04,198
and as long as no law or award term separately requires you to audit your full financial statements.

79
00:05:04,198 --> 00:05:07,336
If all your federal funding flows through one program

80
00:05:07,336 --> 00:05:13,196
your auditor can focus the compliance work on just that program instead of your whole organization.

81
00:05:13,196 --> 00:05:18,643
Most multi-grant nonprofits won't qualify, because they're running several programs at once.

82
00:05:18,643 --> 00:05:23,616
But if you're a smaller shop with one big federal stream, ask your auditor about it.

83
00:05:23,616 --> 00:05:27,819
It's a legitimate, lighter path, and it's written right into the rules.

84
00:05:27,819 --> 00:05:29,498
Okay, you're over a million.

85
00:05:29,498 --> 00:05:30,816
What actually happens.

86
00:05:30,816 --> 00:05:37,231
The first thing you'll build is a document called the Schedule of Expenditures of Federal Awards, the SEFA.

87
00:05:37,231 --> 00:05:44,245
It's exactly what it sounds like: a complete list of every federal program you spent money on this year and how much.

88
00:05:44,245 --> 00:05:46,883
The SEFA is the backbone of the whole audit.

89
00:05:46,883 --> 00:05:49,581
If it's wrong, everything downstream is wrong

90
00:05:49,581 --> 00:05:53,597
and reconstructing it at year-end is where a lot of the pain lives.

91
00:05:53,597 --> 00:05:56,055
Hold that thought, we'll come back to it.

92
00:05:56,055 --> 00:06:01,211
Here's the part that surprises people: the auditor does not test every federal dollar.

93
00:06:01,211 --> 00:06:05,347
They use what's called a risk-based approach to pick certain programs

94
00:06:05,347 --> 00:06:08,465
your "major programs, " for deep compliance testing.

95
00:06:08,465 --> 00:06:13,380
Bigger programs and ones with a history of problems are more likely to get picked.

96
00:06:13,380 --> 00:06:16,378
So you don't get audited evenly across everything.

97
00:06:16,378 --> 00:06:20,095
You get audited where the risk and the money are concentrated.

98
00:06:20,095 --> 00:06:26,809
And to be clear about who runs it: a single audit is performed by an independent CPA firm that you hire and pay.

99
00:06:26,809 --> 00:06:28,967
The government doesn't send someone.

100
00:06:28,967 --> 00:06:33,583
That auditor follows a specific rulebook called Government Auditing Standards

101
00:06:33,583 --> 00:06:35,801
which everyone calls the Yellow Book.

102
00:06:35,801 --> 00:06:42,216
The independence is the point, which is part of why you can't just have your own bookkeeper sign off on it.

103
00:06:42,216 --> 00:06:47,409
When the auditor finds something that doesn't line up, that becomes an audit finding.

104
00:06:47,409 --> 00:06:53,518
Maybe a cost you charged to a grant wasn't allowed, or you couldn't produce a receipt to back it up.

105
00:06:53,518 --> 00:06:59,322
Money the auditor can't verify, or thinks was spent improperly, gets labeled a questioned cost.

106
00:06:59,322 --> 00:07:05,615
And questioned costs are exactly what they sound like: dollars a funder may later ask you to give back.

107
00:07:05,615 --> 00:07:09,403
For every finding, you have to write a corrective action plan.

108
00:07:09,403 --> 00:07:15,818
That's your written answer: here's what went wrong, here's who owns fixing it, here's when it'll be done.

109
00:07:15,818 --> 00:07:18,323
None of this is automatically a disaster.

110
00:07:18,323 --> 00:07:20,339
Clean audits happen all the time.

111
00:07:20,339 --> 00:07:25,532
But findings follow you, because next year's auditor reads last year's findings first

112
00:07:25,532 --> 00:07:30,541
and a pattern of repeat findings is the kind of thing that makes a funder nervous.

113
00:07:30,541 --> 00:07:33,107
There's even an upside to a clean history.

114
00:07:33,107 --> 00:07:37,873
If you've had clean audits and filed them on time for the past couple of years

115
00:07:37,873 --> 00:07:40,988
you may qualify as what's called a low-risk auditee

116
00:07:40,988 --> 00:07:45,632
which means the auditor is allowed to test a smaller slice of your programs.

117
00:07:45,632 --> 00:07:49,786
So the reward for getting it right is a lighter audit the next year.

118
00:07:49,786 --> 00:07:53,392
Once the audit is done, you don't just file it in a drawer.

119
00:07:53,392 --> 00:07:57,915
The whole thing, the financial statements, the SEFA, the auditor's reports

120
00:07:57,915 --> 00:08:00,359
your findings and corrective action plan

121
00:08:00,359 --> 00:08:03,476
plus a summary form called the data collection form

122
00:08:03,476 --> 00:08:11,116
all of that gets bundled into a reporting package and submitted electronically to one place: the Federal Audit Clearinghouse.

123
00:08:11,116 --> 00:08:15,517
If you've filed before and you're hunting for the old Census Bureau site

124
00:08:15,517 --> 00:08:22,362
note that the Clearinghouse moved to the General Services Administration back in October of twenty twenty-three.

125
00:08:22,362 --> 00:08:24,073
It now lives at fac dot gov.

126
00:08:24,073 --> 00:08:27,312
And there's a deadline that catches people off guard.

127
00:08:27,312 --> 00:08:33,791
Your reporting package is due the earlier of two dates: thirty days after you receive the auditor's report

128
00:08:33,791 --> 00:08:38,191
or nine months after the end of your fiscal year, whichever comes first.

129
00:08:38,191 --> 00:08:40,758
So if your year ends December thirty-first

130
00:08:40,758 --> 00:08:45,159
your single audit is generally due by the following September thirtieth.

131
00:08:45,159 --> 00:08:51,087
Miss it, and a late filing is itself the kind of thing that can put you on a funder's watch list.

132
00:08:51,087 --> 00:08:55,399
I build software for this, so let me be honest about where it gets hard

133
00:08:55,399 --> 00:08:58,315
because the rule is simple and the doing is not.

134
00:08:58,315 --> 00:09:01,352
The first problem is just knowing where you stand.

135
00:09:01,352 --> 00:09:04,935
The threshold is about money expended across the whole year

136
00:09:04,935 --> 00:09:08,033
across every federal source including pass-through.

137
00:09:08,033 --> 00:09:12,163
If your federal spending lives in a dozen places, a few spreadsheets

138
00:09:12,163 --> 00:09:14,593
your accounting system, somebody's email

139
00:09:14,593 --> 00:09:19,270
you often can't answer the one question that matters, "did we cross a million

140
00:09:19,270 --> 00:09:22,367
" until you sit down at year-end and add it all up.

141
00:09:22,367 --> 00:09:26,619
By then it's too late to plan for the audit you just learned you need.

142
00:09:26,619 --> 00:09:28,927
The second problem is the SEFA itself.

143
00:09:28,927 --> 00:09:34,272
Rebuilding a complete, accurate schedule of every federal dollar by program, at year-end

144
00:09:34,272 --> 00:09:39,131
from records that weren't kept with the SEFA in mind, is where teams lose weeks.

145
00:09:39,131 --> 00:09:41,136
And the third is the audit trail.

146
00:09:41,136 --> 00:09:45,266
When the auditor asks for the documentation behind a questioned cost

147
00:09:45,266 --> 00:09:51,826
you're either pulling it up in a minute or you're digging through eighteen months of files hoping it exists.

148
00:09:51,826 --> 00:09:54,559
None of that is anyone doing their job badly.

149
00:09:54,559 --> 00:10:00,329
It's what happens when a continuous, year-long tracking job gets treated as a year-end project.

150
00:10:00,329 --> 00:10:02,154
So here's the honest takeaway.

151
00:10:02,154 --> 00:10:06,959
The single audit isn't something to be afraid of, it's something to see coming.

152
00:10:06,959 --> 00:10:10,121
The organizations that handle it well aren't smarter

153
00:10:10,121 --> 00:10:12,737
they just track federal spending as they go

154
00:10:12,737 --> 00:10:17,846
so the SEFA already exists and they always know how close they are to the threshold.

155
00:10:17,846 --> 00:10:26,422
That continuous tracking is exactly the thing I build at GrantPipe: federal expenditures totaled across every fund and pass-through as you go

156
00:10:26,422 --> 00:10:28,611
the audit trail attached to each one

157
00:10:28,611 --> 00:10:32,991
and the SEFA building itself instead of waiting for a year-end scramble.

158
00:10:32,991 --> 00:10:35,606
That's the same structure the rules ask for

159
00:10:35,606 --> 00:10:39,803
kept current by the system instead of by whoever happens to remember.

160
00:10:39,803 --> 00:10:41,688
I'm not going to pitch it here.

161
00:10:41,688 --> 00:10:48,075
If you're nowhere near a million, you may not need any of it yet, and that's a perfectly good answer too.

162
00:10:48,075 --> 00:10:52,697
What I will give you is the checklist version of everything we just covered.

163
00:10:52,697 --> 00:10:57,563
It walks the 2 CFR 200 audit prep: what to gather, what the auditor will ask for

164
00:10:57,563 --> 00:11:02,246
and how to sanity-check your SEFA, so you can see how ready you actually are.

165
00:11:02,246 --> 00:11:02,854
It's free.

166
00:11:02,854 --> 00:11:07,599
Drop your email at the link in the description and I'll send it to your inbox.

167
00:11:07,599 --> 00:11:11,917
And if this made the single audit feel less like a black box, subscribe

168
00:11:11,917 --> 00:11:16,296
because the next video is how to actually prepare for one, step by step.

169
00:11:16,296 --> 00:11:21,831
The version of you whose fiscal year is about to close will be glad you watched this early.
